{
  "dom_id": "step-e13286",
  "name": "Example: Identifying Equivalent Special Sets",
  "problem": "Which of the following sets are equivalent to $A = \\{0, 1, 2, 3, \\ldots\\} ?$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{N}_{0}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}$\n\n- $\\mathbb{Z}^{+}$",
  "worked_solution": "The set $A$ is the set of positive integers and zero. It is denoted by $\\mathbb{N}_{0}$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\mathbb{N}_{0}.$\n\nThe other options are *not* equivalent to $A{:}$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{N}$ does not include the element zero, and $0\\in A.$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}$ includes the elements $-1,-2,-3,\\ldots,$ and $-1 \\not \\in A, -2\\notin A, -3\\notin A, \\ldots\\,.$\n\n- The set $\\mathbb{Z}^{+}$ does not include the element zero, and $0\\in A.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "result": null,
